we begin this half-hour with a  controversial roundup wild horses have  long been a feature of the American West  but now hundreds are being removed from  a remote section of Northern California  because as often happens on western  lands they're caught up in the crossfire  of competing interests John Blackstone  has the story the helicopter over a  rocky plateau in Northern California is  doing the work of a modern cowboy  rounding up wild horses ending their  days of running free we are sending 700  animals down to Corral's for adoption  training try and get them homes where  people can use them and ride them Amanda  McAdams supervisor of Modoc National  Forest says she's received thousands of  emails objecting to the roundup removing  horses from the land is going to make it  a better situation for the horses that  remain out there for these horses part  of the reason they're called standing  behind us is they have food and water  they're not having to go searching for  it nearly four thousand wild horses now  live in Modoc National Forest there's  really room for 200 to 400 to exist  healthy in balance with the land the  land where the wild horses live is  called the devil's garden a landscape of  volcanic rocks where the grass is often  over grazed by horses how high should  this be there's some examples around  here where it's probably three or four  inches but there's just no vegetation  left and you can see how they're just  dying out because of over grazing this  land has been closed for use by cattle  ranchers who are now suing the Forest  Service a lot of these areas don't have  cattle on them now and the degradation  is still occurring but the horses have  ardent defenders far from the open range  the American wild horse campaign has  been holding fundraisers fearing some of  the horses will ultimately be  slaughtered in the average markets for  horse meat at auction horses are going  for hundreds of dollars apiece so when  they when the Forest Service says that  they're going to sell them for a dollar  a piece we know they are going to  slaughter  the Forest Service admits some older  horses could be sold for as little as a  dollar and they have no way of knowing  what happens to those animals but even  as the horses are being gathered some  enthusiastic buyers are looking for  animals they would love to adopt you saw  some special ones there today  pretty colors out there  Janie Denis and Catherine have all  adopted wild horses in the past  Catherines was captured at devil's  garden last year and he's four years old  now and he's a great horse I mean I  couldn't ask for a better horse the  horses that are gathered get to go to  homes or there they're hopefully never  in need of anything anymore I'd rather  see them going to homes than starve to  death out on the range cuz they will  it's a controversy that won't end soon  even if the Forest Service brings in a  thousand horses this year as planned  another roundup will be needed next year  for CBS this morning Saturday John  Blackstone Modoc national forests  they're expensive they are really  expensive I came this close to owning a  horse my dad in Compton California of  all places  but yeah they're just the upkeep  tremendous well I hope those horses find  a home I do too
 

